
  Three failure modes, and they need different answers.

  [1mCorrupt or foreign storage.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is shared across everything on the origin, and the key can hold anything —
  truncated JSON, a value written by an older version, a string where an object belongs. Every read goes through one
  private [38;5;153mreadRecord()[39m that wraps [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m in try/catch and validates the shape ([38;5;153muserId[39m a non-empty string, [38;5;153mexpiresAt[39m
  a finite number). Anything that fails validation is treated as absent and the key is removed. The rule: a bad record
  never throws out of [38;5;153mget()[39m and never half-loads. [38;5;153mget()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m or a valid userId — no third state.

  [1mStorage unavailable.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws on access in Safari private mode and when the quota is full — including on
  [3mwrite[0m, which is the case people forget. [38;5;153mset()[39m catches, keeps the userId in an in-memory fallback for the page's
  lifetime, and returns a boolean so the caller knows persistence didn't happen. Degrading to "works until you close the
  tab" beats an uncaught exception in a submit handler.

  [1mExpiry — and here's the honest limit.[0m [38;5;153mget()[39m compares [38;5;153mDate.now()[39m against [38;5;153mexpiresAt[39m; past it, the record is cleared and
  [38;5;153mnull[39m returned. Default TTL 24h, a named constant.

  This is a [3mcleanliness[0m mechanism, not a security one, and the spec will say so in those words. Three reasons it can't
  be more: the clock is the user's and can be moved; the record is in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m where any XSS or devtools session can
  rewrite [38;5;153mexpiresAt[39m; and the TTL is the client's guess about a session lifetime only the server knows. The actual
  expiry that matters is the HttpOnly cookie's, which this code can't read by design.

  So the contract I'd write down: [1m[38;5;153mAppIdentity.get()[39m returning a userId means "this browser recently saw a login", never
[0m  [1m"this request is authorized."[0m Authorization is the server re-checking the session cookie on every call. The TTL exists
  so a shared machine doesn't show a stale name in the UI a week later — not to keep anyone out.

  [1mThe gap this leaves, stated plainly.[0m Between the cookie expiring and the TTL lapsing, [38;5;153mget()[39m returns a userId for a
  session that's already dead. With no backend there is no way to detect that — the only real fix is a 401 handler, and
  there are no network calls to get a 401 from. The spec will carry this as a named limitation with the resolution
  attached: when the real API lands, every non-2xx auth response calls [38;5;153mclear()[39m. I'd rather write that down than let the
  TTL imply a correctness it doesn't have.

  Does the error handling look right?

